WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.430
Three bad days are coming.

2
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:03.830
A region goes dark.

3
00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.880
A bad update wipes the prices.

4
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.365
And a partner feed shows up late.

5
00:00:08.385 --> 00:00:12.090
Design the platform so each one is a non-event.

6
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:14.925
First: assume everything fails sometimes.

7
00:00:14.945 --> 00:00:16.875
When a call fails, retry.

8
00:00:16.895 --> 00:00:19.860
But wait longer each time, with a little randomness.

9
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:24.270
If everyone retries every second, they knock the service down again.

10
00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.330
Make writes safe to repeat, so a retry never doubles the data.

11
00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.055
Save your place with checkpoints.

12
00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:32.665
Then a restart picks up where it stopped.

13
00:00:32.685 --> 00:00:35.490
And send poison messages to a dead-letter queue.

14
00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.040
The pipeline keeps going.

15
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.425
Second: a whole location fails.

16
00:00:40.445 --> 00:00:42.137
A zone is one building.

17
00:00:42.157 --> 00:00:44.910
A region is a city with several buildings.

18
00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:47.010
Most managed services are regional.

19
00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:50.070
They survive one zone failing, on their own.

20
00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.280
A whole region failing needs two things from you.

21
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.730
Data that is copied to another region.

22
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:59.105
And a way to start the compute again over there.

23
00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:01.565
Third: someone runs a bad update.

24
00:01:01.585 --> 00:01:03.802
Do not reach for last night's backup yet.

25
00:01:03.822 --> 00:01:06.050
BigQuery has time travel.

26
00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.885
You can read any table as it was, up to 7 days ago.

27
00:01:09.905 --> 00:01:12.310
Other undo buttons: table snapshots,

28
00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.995
object versioning in Cloud Storage, and seek in Pub/Sub.

29
00:01:16.015 --> 00:01:18.357
And always keep the raw data unchanged.

30
00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:21.020
Then you can rebuild everything.

31
00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.208
For the late partner feed: wait for the data with a sensor,

32
00:01:24.228 --> 00:01:26.655
check the row count, then backfill.

33
00:01:26.675 --> 00:01:29.992
Cloud SQL high availability fails over by itself.

34
00:01:30.012 --> 00:01:33.140
A cross-region replica needs you to promote it.

35
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:35.428
And test your failover before the bad day.

36
00:01:35.448 --> 00:01:37.365
Not during it.

37
00:01:37.385 --> 00:01:37.990
Recap.

38
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.850
The last one of the course.

39
00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.162
Retry with backoff.

40
00:01:41.182 --> 00:01:42.513
Write idempotently.

41
00:01:42.533 --> 00:01:44.635
Replicate across regions.

42
00:01:44.655 --> 00:01:46.760
Keep an undo button, like time travel.

43
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.595
And test your failover.

44
00:01:48.615 --> 00:01:50.133
That was the last station.

45
00:01:50.153 --> 00:01:53.715
Finish the checkpoint, then face the boss.
